<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benevento</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Beneventum, Maleventum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benevento is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in a valley of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>. Originally the Samnite town of Maleventum, it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony of Beneventum in 268 BCE and a station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Via Appia</a>, with the Arch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>. From 571 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> duchy of Benevento, and later it belonged to the Papal States until 1860.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benevento.jpg" alt="Benevento" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio De Capua (original pictures: Phil Tizzani, Antonio De Capua, Miguel Hermoso Cuesta) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benevento-collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pyrrhus of Epirus won costly victories at Heraclea in 280 BCE and Asculum in 279 BCE but withdrew after <b>Beneventum</b> in 275 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Near <b>Benevento</b> in southern Italy, the army of Charles I of Anjou defeated Manfred, King of Sicily, who was killed in the battle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-26">26 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghibellines then held Florence until after the Angevin victory at <b>Benevento</b> in 1266.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-4">4 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Lombard warriors under Zotto established themselves at <b>Beneventum</b> in Campania around 571, perhaps first as soldiers in Byzantine pay.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-benevento">Duchy of Benevento</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy fought the Lombards of <b>Benevento</b>, but sometimes allied with Arab raiders, earning papal condemnation.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-naples">Duchy of Naples</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 9th century, amid the conflicts with Lombard <b>Benevento</b> and Arab raiders, it gained self-government under its own dukes, like neighbouring Amalfi.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-sorrento">Duchy of Sorrento</a></li></ul></section>
